Abstract
In this work, water-stable Al-MIL-101 analogues were successfully synthesized by adjusting the Fe/Al ratio to obtain excellent phosphorus removal efficiencies. The introduction of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ into the precursor solution allowed the final structure of aluminum metal-organic frameworks   (Al-MOFs) to be tuned without introducing Fe into the final structure. The formation of Al-MIL-101 analogues with different morphologies and surface areas was accomplished by adjusting the Fe/Al molar ratio in the precursor solution. Compared with pure Fe-MIL-101 or Al-MIL-101, Al-MIL-101 analogues exhibited ultra-fast phosphorous adsorption kinetics and high phosphorous adsorption capacities. Al-MIL-101, produced with an $\mathrm{Fe/Al}$ feed molar ratio of 0.5, achieved a maximum phosphorus uptake capacity of $90{\mathrm{~mg~P/g}}$ , which is much higher than the phosphorus absorption reported in most literatures. More importantly, the Al-MIL-101 analogue obtained using an Fe/Al molar ratio of 0.5 exhibited an excellent phosphorus removal efficiency, even after multiple adsorption/desorption cycles. These results indicate that Al-MOFs produced by adjusting the Fe amount in the precursor solution are promising candidates for the removal of phosphate from water. Keywords: phosphorus; metal-organic frameworks; morphology-tunable; adsorption
## 1. Introduction
Phosphorus is one of the elements necessary to maintain balanced ecosystems, but excessive phosphorus emissions may result in the eutrophication of aquatic environments [1-3]. In lakes, studies have shown that accelerated eutrophication occurs at phosphorus concentrations exceeding $0.02\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{P}/\mathrm{L}$ . This is accompanied by the excessive growth of vegetation and algae, water quality deterioration, and aquatic ecosystem destruction, which all ultimately endanger human health [4]. Therefore, many government agencies have enacted strict regulations for phosphorus concentrations in lakes. For instance, the US Environmental Protection Agency (US EPA) recommends that the total phosphorus concentration of any river should not exceed $0.05\:\mathrm{mg\P/L}$ if it flows into a lake or reservoir [5]. The maximum phosphorus concentration to prevent the eutrophication of lakes has been set by the European Union (EU) at ${<0.01\ \mathrm{mg\P/L}}$ [5]. Therefore, the efficient removal of phosphorus from aquatic environments is especially important in water treatment. In the EU, the Water Framework Directive has reduced the allowable phosphorus concentration in wastewater treatment plant emissions to $0.1\mathrm{{\mg\P/L}}$ [6]. As phosphorus emission standards become more stringent, it is imperative to develop more efficient phosphorus removal strategies. In view of its simple design, low cost, and high efficiency, adsorption is preferred over chemical precipitation, biological treatment, and membrane separation [7-9]. To date, many adsorbents have been applied for the removal of phosphates, including graphite [10], mesoporous silicon nanoparticles [11], red mud [12], and organic polymers [13]. However, the non-specific interaction between these adsorbents and phosphates generally results in poor selectivity. At the same time, since metal sites in metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) can specifically bind phosphate ions in solution, MOFs are strongly favored by researchers [13, 14]. Porous MOFs are promising candidates for phosphorus purification due to their unique structures and physicochemical characteristics, including abundant metal active sites, adjustable pore sizes, large surface areas, and customizable functions [15].
In particular, Al-MOFs have received significant attention due to their abundant metal source, strong thermal stability, and excellent water stability. Gándara et al. showed that two novel AlMOFs exhibited high methane storage capacities [16]. Meanwhile, Al-SA MOF was able to efficiently adsorb acid black 1 (AB1) in aqueous media [17], and other studies have shown that MIL-96 exhibits excellent fluorine removal from drinking water [18]. Although the preparation of aluminum-based MOFs has made great progress, many significant challenges remain to feasibly manufacture Al-MOFs with custom properties.
In this work, a series of Al-MIL-101 analogues with excellent water stability, different morphologies, sizes, and surface areas were synthesized by introducing $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ into the precursor solution. The inorganic framework nodes of these adsorbents were Al and did not contain Fe. In addition, the effect of the Fe/Al molar ratio on the microstructural characteristics of the adsorbent were investigated, which helped optimize the phosphate absorption performance. The adsorption kinetics and equilibrium adsorption isotherms, as well as the effects of initial pH, coexisting ions, and other key factors on phosphate adsorption were discussed in detail. XPS and FT-IR spectroscopy were used to elucidate the probable phosphate removal mechanism of Al-MIL-101 analogues. Finally, the feasibility of using Al-MIL-101 analogues to remove phosphate from real water samples was evaluated.
## 2. Experiment
## 2.1. Chemicals and materials
All of the reagents used in the experiment were analytical reagent grade and were used without further purification. Ferric chloride hexahydrate $(\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}.6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ , HCl $(35-37\%)$ , 1,4- benzenedicarboxly acid ( $\mathrm{\cdotH}_{2}\mathrm{BDC})$ ) and aluminum chloride $(\mathrm{AlCl}_{3})$ ) were purchased from Tianjin Sailboat Chemical Reagent Technology Co., Ltd. (Tianjin, China). Sodium hydroxide (NaOH) and potassium dihydrogen phosphate $\mathrm{\KH_{2}P O_{4}},$ ) were obtained from Tianjin Chemical Reagent Company (Tianjin, China). Calcium chloride $\mathrm{(CaCl}_{2})$ , sodium nitrate $\mathrm{(NaNO_{3})}$ , magnesium chloride $\mathrm{(MgCl}_{2})$ ), N, N-Dimethylformamide (DMF) and sodium chloride (NaCl) were obtained from Xilong Chemical Co., Ltd. Sodium bicarbonate $\mathrm{(NaHCO_{3})}$ , sodium carbonate $\left(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}\right)$ , and sodium sulfate 1 $\mathrm{(Na_{2}S O_{4,})}$ were purchased from Tianjin Yongda Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. (Tianjin, China).
## 2.2. Preparation of adsorbents
Al-MIL-101 analogues were fabricated using a strategy reported by Karthik et al. with slight modifications [19]. Briefly, 5 mmol of a metal salt with a molar ratio of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}/\mathrm{Al}^{3+}$ of 2:1, 1:1, or 1:2, and 2 mmol of terephthalic acid $\mathrm{\langleH_{2}B D C)}$ were dissolved in $60~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF. After sonication for 30 min, a homogeneous solution was obtained and subsequently treated at $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $20\mathrm{h}$ in a 100 mL Teflon-lined autoclave. After the mixture was cooled to room temperature, the obtained solid was collected and washed several times with DMF. Thereafter, the sample was dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 30 min and continued to be activated at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 12 hours. The three products were labeled Fe/Al (n) (Fe/Al (2), Fe/Al (1), and Fe/Al (0.5)), where $n$ represents the feed molar ratio of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}/\mathrm{Al}^{3+}$ in the precursor solution. Fe-MIL-101 and Al-MIL-101 were also synthesized by a similar method.
## 2.3. Phosphates adsorption/removal experiment
To determine the maximum phosphate adsorption capacity of different adsorbents, an adsorbent $(0.006\ \mathrm{g)}$ was mixed with $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of a phosphate solution at an initial concentration ranging from 5 to $200\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{P/L}$ in a centrifuge tube. The solution was continuously shaken at $200\ \mathrm{rpm}$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ in a constant-temperature shaker $(25~\mathrm{^\circC}$ ). Thereafter, the mixed solution was taken out and filtered through a $0.45\upmu\mathrm{m}$ membrane to determine the phosphate concentration using the molybdenum blue method [20]. Equation (1) was used to calculate the phosphate adsorption capacity.
$$
q_{e}=\left(C_{o}-C_{e}\right)_{\mathrm{~m~}}^{V}
$$
where $C_{\theta}$ ( $\bf{\dot{m g}\Delta P/L}$ ) is the initial phosphorus concentration, $C_{e}$ $\mathrm{{\stackrel{\cdot}{m g}}\ P/L}$ ) is the equilibrium phosphorus concentration, $V(\mathrm{L})$ is the total volume of the mixed solution, and $m$ (g) is the adsorbent mass.
To determine the phosphate adsorption kinetics, an adsorbent $(0.006\ \mathrm{g})$ was thoroughly mixed with $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of a phosphate solution $\mathrm{5\mg\P/L})$ ) at $298\mathrm{K},$ , an initial $\mathsf{p H}$ of 5.5, and a mixing speed of $200~\mathrm{rpm}$ . To examine the effect of initial pH on the phosphorus removal efficiency, $0.006\ \mathrm{g}$ of adsorbent was suspended in $30\mathrm{mL}$ of phosphate solution (pH, 3.0-11.0). Other parameters included: a temperature of $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , an initial phosphate concentration of ${5}\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{P/L}$ , and a mixing speed of 200 rpm. Equation (2) was used to calculate the phosphate removal rate.
$$
\mathrm{~R~}(\%)=\frac{C_{0}-C_{e}}{C_{0}}\mathrm{~\times~}100\%
$$
Batch experiments were conducted to study the effects of coexisting ions. Adsorbent (0.006 g) was mixed with $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of phosphorus solution $S\operatorname{mg}\mathrm{P/L}$ ). Then, NaCl, ${\mathrm{NaNO}}_{3}$ , ${\mathrm{Na}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ , ${\mathrm{NaHCO}}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{CaCl}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{MgCl}_{2}$ , were added to the phosphorus solution to introduce $50~\mathrm{mg/L}$ of Cl-, $\mathrm{NO}_{3}\mathrm{\cdot}$ , ${\mathrm{SO}}_{4}{}^{2\cdot}$ , $\mathrm{CO}_{3}{}^{2\cdot}$ -, ${\mathrm{HCO}}_{3}{\cdot}$ -, $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ , and $\mathrm{Mg^{2+}}$ , respectively, and mixed for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ at $200\mathrm{rpm}$ .
## 2.4. Desorption and regeneration studies
To evaluate the regenerative capacity of the adsorbent, adsorbents were added to a $5~\mathrm{{mg}~P/L}$ phosphate solution at a concentration of $0.2~\mathrm{g/L}$ , and phosphate-loaded adsorbents were prepared by mixing at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{5\mathrm{h}}$ . The used adsorbents were collected by filtration, and then dried before being immersed in a sodium chloride solution (NaCl, $1\%\mathrm{w/v}$ ) at a dose of $0.2\ \mathrm{g/L}$ and reacted at $298{\mathrm{~K~}}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ for desorption experiments. The obtained MOF was then dried and re-used for additional phosphate adsorption.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Morphological and structural characterization
Fig. 1 shows characteristic SEM images of synthesized materials. Pure Al-MIL-101 exhibited a pineapple-like morphology (Fig. 1a) with an average size of $1{-}3~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . Pure Fe-MIL-101 exhibits a clear octahedral structure (Fig. 1c) with an average size of ca. $500\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which is consistent with previously-reported data [21]. In addition, the Fe/Al (2), Fe/Al (1) and Fe/Al (0.5) frameworks exhibited uniform rice-like, cylinders and lantern-like morphologies (Fig. 1d, e, i), respectively. In addition, when the Al content in the precursor solution was higher, the Al-MIL-101 analogues were larger. Thus, it can be reasonably inferred that different morphologies and sizes of Fe/Al (n) frameworks could be obtained by controlling the feed ratio of Fe to Al. In addition, Jiang et al. found that the coordination between different metal ions and ligands may affect the nucleation and growth of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathbf{M}\cdot$ -MOF crystals [22]. Meanwhile, the corresponding transmission electron microscope (TEM) images further confirmed that the morphologies of Al-MIL-101 (Fig. 1b), Fe-MIL-101 (Fig. 1f), Fe/Al (2) (Fig. 1g), Fe/Al (1) (Fig. 1h) and Fe/Al (0.5) (Fig. 1j) were consistent with the SEM images. The EDX spectroscopy (Fig. S1) analysis of Fe/Al (0.5) showed that the Al $w t\%$ in the Fe/Al (0.5) framework was significantly higher than the Fe $w t\%$ , which was hardly detected.
However, the Fe/Al (0.5) framework was significantly different from that of Al-MIL-101, which shows that the addition of $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ to the precursor solution formed Al-MIL-101 analogues with different morphologies and diameters.
TEM images of various samples: (b) Al-MOF, (f) Fe-MOF, (g) Fe/Al (2), (h) Fe/Al (1), (j) Fe/Al (0.5).
XRD was used to examine the crystal phases of different adsorbents, and XRD patterns of pure Fe-MIL-101 and Al-MIL-101 were also provided for comparison [21]. As shown in Fig. 2, all patterns showed sharp and prominent peaks, indicating that the synthesized MOFs were highly crystalline. In addition, the intensity of the Fe-MIL-101 characteristic diffraction peak was slightly less than that of Al-MIL-101 and Fe/Al (n). The peak position of Fe/Al (n) was very similar to that of pure Al-MIL-101, but not to that of Fe-MIL-101, although their intensities were somewhat similar, indicating that Fe/Al (n) and Al-MIL-101 had the same skeletal topology.
The surface area and porosity of synthesized MOFs were analyzed by nitrogen adsorption/desorption tests at $77~\mathrm{K}$ . As shown in Fig. 3a, all adsorbents showed typical type I isotherms, indicating highly microporous structures. The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface areas of Fe-MIL-101, Al-MIL-101, Fe/Al (2), Fe/Al (1), and Fe/Al (0.5) are 374, 1280, 1339, 1491, and $1180\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ , respectively. Surprisingly, the surface area of Fe/Al (n) was significantly different from that of Al-MIL-101, indicating that the introduction of Fe into the precursor solution of synthetic Al-MIL-101 analogues increased the number of exposed Fe/Al (n) active sites.
In addition, the thermal stability of Fe/Al (n) was examined. It can be seen from Fig. 3b that as the concentration of Al in the precursor solution increased, the decomposition temperature of the synthesized Fe/Al (n) framework also gradually increased. The special structure, large surface area, and high thermal decomposition temperature of Fe/Al (n) suggest it will be an excellent material for the removal of phosphate in water.
## 3.2. Sorption isotherm and kinetics
The maximum phosphorous adsorption capacities of Fe-MIL-101, Al-MIL-101, and Fe/Al (n) were obtained by varying the initial phosphorus concentration. Fig. 4 shows that the amount of adsorbed phosphorus increased as the phosphate concentration in the solution increased, before gradually plateauing. Fe-MIL-101 and Al-MIL-101 adsorbed relatively low amounts of phosphorus, and adsorption capacity of Fe/Al (n) for phosphorus increased after Fe was introduced into the precursor solution. The maximum phosphorus adsorption by Fe/Al (0.5) was $90\ \mathrm{mg\P/g}$ , far exceeding the previously reported phosphate adsorbents listed in Table 1. In addition, the Langmuir and Friedrich models were used to analyze the phosphorous adsorption capacity of different adsorbents, and the isothermal equations are expressed as follows (Eqs. (3) and (4)):
$$
\frac{C_{e}}{q_{e}}=\frac{1}{b q_{e}}+\frac{C_{e}}{q_{m}}
$$
$$
\begin{array}{r}{\log q_{e}=l o g K+\frac{1}{n}l o g C_{e}}\end{array}
$$
where $q_{e}$ $\mathrm{(mg/g)}$ and $C_{e}$ $(\mathrm{mg/L})$ are the phosphorus capacity and phosphorus concentration at adsorption equilibrium, respectively, $\boldsymbol{b}_{\mathrm{~\tiny~(L/mg)~}}$ represents the Langmuir adsorption equilibrium constant, and $q_{m}\left(\mathrm{mg/g}\right)$ is the maximum adsorption capacity. The value of $K[\mathrm{(mol/g)/(mol/L)^{1/n}}]$ is the amount of absorbed phosphorus, and $I/n$ is an indicator of adsorption strength.
In addition, the corresponding fitting curve is presented in Fig. S2, and the correlation coefficients are shown in Table S1. Since the correlation coefficient of the Langmuir model was higher than that of the Freundlich model, the Langmuir model better describes the corresponding isotherm data. This indicates a uniform distribution of both the single-layer phosphate adsorption mode and adsorption sites on the obtained adsorbent.
The phosphorus removal kinetics of various absorbents was also evaluated to further demonstrate the advantages of the Fe/Al (n) materials. Fig. 5 shows the time dependence of phosphorus removal at an initial concentration of $5~\mathrm{mg/L}$ and an adsorbent dose of $0.2~\mathrm{g/L}$ . Fe-MOF and Al-MOF removed phosphorus relatively slowly before reaching an equilibrium at 100 minutes. However, the absorption of phosphorus on $\mathrm{Fe/Al}$ (n) was quite fast during the initial $20~\mathrm{min}$ . Notably, when the same mass of adsorbent was added to a solution with an identical volume and initial phosphorus concentration, the phosphorus removal efficiency increased as the Fe/Al molar ratio in the precursor solution decreased in Fe/Al (n). Generally, Fe/Al (0.5) showed a phosphorus removal efficiency of almost $99\%$ . In order to gain a deeper understanding of the phosphate adsorption process by different adsorbents, pseudo-first and pseudo second-order kinetic models were used to fit the kinetic data, as follows (Eqs. (5) and (6)) [35]:
$$
\ln\left(q_{e}-q_{t}\right)=l n q_{e}-k_{1}t
$$
$$
\frac{t}{q_{t}}=\frac{1}{k_{2}q_{e}^{2}}+\frac{t}{q_{e}}
$$
where $k_{I}(\mathrm{min}^{-1})$ and $k_{2}(\mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{mg}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}^{-1})$ represent the pseudo first-order and pseudo second-order rate constants, respectively, and $q_{e}$ and $q_{t}$ represent the equilibrium adsorption capacity and adsorption capacity at time $t$ , respectively.
The fitted kinetic constants and correlation coefficients $(R^{2})$ are shown in Table S2. For many adsorbents, $R^{2}$ was greater than 0.99 for the pseudo-second-order model, although the pseudo firstorder kinetic model also fit well. Similar results were observed for phosphorus removal by other materials, such as MIL-101 MOFs [21], zirconium-based metal organic frameworks [36], and cubic zeolite immediate framework-8 [28]. At the same time, the $q_{e}$ values obtained using the pseudo second-order model was similar to the experimentally-determined equilibrium adsorption capacities (Fig. S3).
Fig. 5. Adsorption kinetics of different absorbents. (Initial $\mathrm{~\bf~P~}$ concentration: $5\mathrm{mg/L}$ , adsorbent dosage: $0.2\ {\mathrm{g/L}}$ , $\mathrm{T}=$ 298K.)
## 3.3. Influence of initial pH on phosphorus removal
Many experimental studies have shown that the $\mathfrak{p H}$ of water is one of the most influential factors affecting actual water treatment. To determine the optimum pH for phosphorus adsorption, the phosphorus removal efficiency was examined in the pH range of 3-11 using Fe-MIL-101, Al-MIL101, and optimized Fe/Al (0.5) as representative adsorbents. The initial phosphorus concentration was $5~\mathrm{mg/L}$ . As shown in Fig. 6a, the three adsorbents exhibited excellent phosphorus removal performances $590\%$ absorption efficiencies) over a pH range of 3-9. When the initial pH was 9 to 11, the adsorption efficiency was significantly lower, and this behavior has also been observed in other MOFs used to adsorb phosphate [28]. However, most contaminated natural waters have a pH between 6.0- 8.5 [37], and the phosphorous removal efficiency of the adsorbents was very high in this pH range.
In addition, the equilibrium pH was determined. As shown in Fig. 6b, when the initial pH changed from 5 to 11, the equilibrium pH was slightly less than the initial pH, indicating that ligand exchange may have occurred during phosphorus adsorption [37]. These results suggest that these adsorbents have a wide pH operating range and excellent phosphorous removal capabilities, and that they will be effective in phosphorus remediation in most naturally contaminated waters.
