## 3.4. Influence of coexisting species on phosphorus removal
From a practical point of view, real wastewater is usually a mixture of various coexisting ions such as Cl-, $\mathrm{NO}_{3}\cdot$ -, ${\mathrm{SO}}_{4}{}^{2\cdot}$ -, $\mathrm{CO}_{3}{}^{2\cdot}$ , $\mathrm{HCO}_{3}\cdot$ , $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ , and $\mathrm{Mg^{2+}}$ , and the anions especially compete with phosphate ions for adsorption sites. Therefore, the adsorbents should also have a specific selectivity for the phosphorus oxyanion [38]. To evaluate the phosphate selectivity of Fe-MIL-101, Al-MIL101, and Fe/Al (0.5), phosphorus removal experiments were conducted in the presence of specific coexisting ions. For this study, the initial concentration of phosphate was kept constant at $5\mathrm{mg/L}$ , while the concentration of other ions was kept constant at $50~\mathrm{mg/L}$ , which are the average typical concentrations of anions and cations in actual wastewater [39]. It is evident from Fig. 7 that the interference of common ions such as Cl-, $\mathrm{NO}_{3}\cdot$ , $\mathrm{SO}_{4}{}^{2\cdot}$ , $\mathrm{CO}_{3}{}^{2}$ -, ${\mathrm{HCO}}_{3}{\cdot}$ , $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ , and $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ is negligible, even though they were present in a concentration 10 times higher than that of the phosphorus. This indicates that Fe-MIL-101, Al-MIL-101, and Fe/Al (0.5) have a high phosphorous selectivity, in agreement with previously reported findings regarding the removal of phosphate by Fe-MOF and Al-MOF materials such as $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101 [21]. This strong selectivity promotes the recovery of high-purity phosphate by the regenerative depletion of Fe-MIL-101, Al-MIL-101, and Fe/Al (0.5).
