## 3.5. Recyclability of Fe/Al (0.5) and phosphate removal from natural water
Four consecutive phosphate adsorption/desorption cycles were carried out to evaluate the reusability of Fe/Al (0.5). Regeneration was carried out by immersing used Fe/Al (0.5) in a $1\%$ w/v sodium chloride solution for 24 hours [21]. Rinsed and dried Fe/Al (0.5) was used for continuous phosphate adsorption/desorption cycles. As shown in Fig. 8, the phosphorus removal efficiency remained above $76\%$ , even after four adsorption-desorption cycles, suggesting that Fe/Al (0.5) had promising potential for use as a recovery/recycle platform.
Water samples were taken from Dianchi Lake (Kunming, China) to demonstrate the feasibility of using Fe/Al (0.5) to remove phosphorus $\mathrm{0.074~mg~P/L}$ ) from natural waters. The water samples were used directly without any treatment. As shown in Fig. 9a, as the amount of Fe/Al (0.5) increased, the concentration of residual phosphorus gradually decreased. When the Fe/Al (0.5)
dosage was only $0.02~\mathrm{g/L}$ , the residual phosphorus concentration was approximately $0.02~\mathrm{mg/L}$ . The effect of adsorption time on phosphorus removal using $\mathrm{Fe/Al}$ (0.5) in natural water samples was investigated. After absorbents $(0.04~\mathrm{g/L})$ were injected into water samples (Fig. 9b), the phosphorous removal efficiency rapidly increased and reached an adsorption equilibrium within 60 minutes, with a removal rate of $94.6\%$ . At this time, the residual phosphorus concentration in the water was only $0.004\mathrm{{mg/L}}$ , which was far lower than the phosphorus concentration that causes lake eutrophication $(0.020\mathrm{mg/L})$ . The rapid and efficient phosphorus removal further demonstrated the applicability of the developed $\mathrm{Fe/Al}$ (0.5) in complex real water bodies, indicating its potential for purifying phosphorous-contaminated water.
