## 3.7. Mechanism of phosphorus removal on the Fe/Al (0.5)
To investigate the phosphate adsorption mechanism of Fe/Al (0.5), FT-IR spectra were used to analyze the surface functional groups of Fe/Al (0.5) before and after phosphorus removal (Fig. 10a). After phosphorous adsorption, the hydroxyl group stretching vibration near $3436~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ was weakened. Compared with the original material, two new peaks appeared at 730.11 and 1276.57 $\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ and were assigned to the protonated P-O-H group of the adsorbed phosphorus species and the bending vibration of P-O, respectively [21]. This data provides strong evidence that adsorbed phosphorus was removed from water primarily through ligand exchange and chemical adsorption. To further elucidate the phosphorus removal mechanism, Fe/Al (0.5) was analyzed by XPS before and after phosphorus removal. Compared with the wide-scan XPS spectra of as-prepared Fe/Al (0.5) (Fig. 10b), the appearance of the P2p peak after phosphorus removal demonstrated the successful adsorption of phosphate onto the adsorbents. As shown in the high-resolution XPS P2p spectra in Fig. 10c, the shift from $134.58\mathrm{eV}$ to $135.38\mathrm{eV}$ indicates that a strong chemical bond was formed between phosphate and Fe/Al (0.5), compared with the reference spectrum of ${\mathrm{KH}}_{2}{\mathrm{PO}}_{4}$ (about 134.0 eV). Furthermore, as shown in Fig. 10d, the Al 2p spectrum shifted slightly towards a lower binding energy from $75.38\mathrm{eV}$ to $74.78\mathrm{eV}$ after phosphorus adsorption, indicating possible electron transfer in the valence band of Al 2p and the formation of an Al-O-P inner-sphere complex. In the O1s spectra of Fe/Al (0.5) after phosphate uptake, deconvoluted peaks appeared, which were attributed to O at 531.73 eV (Al-O-P and $\scriptstyle\mathrm{P=O}$ ), 532.54 eV (P-O-H), and 533.36 eV (O- $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ ) (Fig. 10f) [40]. The appearance of O species after phosphorous adsorption confirmed that ligand exchange had occurred, and that an Al-O-P coordination bond had formed on Fe/Al (0.5) MOF, consistent with the FTIR analysis.
